  "claim": "The nickname of the English monarch that Petruccio Ubaldini presented one of his books was The Virgin Queen.",
  "evidencia": "Page: Petruccio Ubaldini. Sentence: Petruccio Ubaldini (ca.1524–ca.1600) was an Italian (\"Tuscan\") mercenary soldier for Henry VIII (1545–47) and in Edward VI's Scottish war (1549) a calligraphist and illuminator on vellum, who was working in England in the reign of Queen Elizabeth, and seems to have enjoyed the favor of the Court.\nPage: Petruccio Ubaldini. Sentence: One of his illuminated books, presented by him to Elizabeth, is in the Bodleian Library, and Walpole gives a list of other works, formerly in the King's Library, and now, most of them, in the British Museum.\nPage: Elizabeth I of England. Sentence: Elizabeth I (7 September 1533 – 24 March 1603) was Queen of England and Ireland from 17 November 1558 until her death.\nPage: Elizabeth I of England. Sentence: Sometimes called The Virgin Queen, Gloriana or Good Queen Bess, Elizabeth was the last monarch of the House of Tudor.",
  "evidence_text": "Page: Petruccio Ubaldini. Sentence: Petruccio Ubaldini (ca.1524–ca.1600) was an Italian (\"Tuscan\") mercenary soldier for Henry VIII (1545–47) and in Edward VI's Scottish war (1549) a calligraphist and illuminator on vellum, who was working in England in the reign of Queen Elizabeth, and seems to have enjoyed the favor of the Court.\nPage: Petruccio Ubaldini. Sentence: One of his illuminated books, presented by him to Elizabeth, is in the Bodleian Library, and Walpole gives a list of other works, formerly in the King's Library, and now, most of them, in the British Museum.\nPage: Elizabeth I of England. Sentence: Elizabeth I (7 September 1533 – 24 March 1603) was Queen of England and Ireland from 17 November 1558 until her death.\nPage: Elizabeth I of England. Sentence: Sometimes called The Virgin Queen, Gloriana or Good Queen Bess, Elizabeth was the last monarch of the House of Tudor.",
